WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.428
Input sources in JavaScript span both the browser and the server.

00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:08.363
In the browser, visible form fields are only the beginning.

00:00:08.363 --> 00:00:11.904
Query strings, route parameters, hidden fields, cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage,

00:00:11.904 --> 00:00:15.839
postMessage events, file uploads, drag-and-drop data, pasted content, browser extensions,

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:19.380
and responses from APIs can all influence application behavior.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.102
A value being present in the browser does not make it trustworthy.

00:00:24.102 --> 00:00:26.069
On the server side, Node.

00:00:26.069 --> 00:00:30.004
js applications commonly process request bodies, headers, cookies, path parameters,

00:00:30.004 --> 00:00:33.938
query parameters, JSON APIs, GraphQL operations, WebSocket messages, uploaded files,

00:00:33.938 --> 00:00:37.873
queue messages, scheduled job data, configuration files, and environment variables.

00:00:37.873 --> 00:00:43.382
Some of these inputs may feel internal, but they can still be malformed, stale,

00:00:43.382 --> 00:00:46.136
compromised, or outside the current application contract.

00:00:46.136 --> 00:00:47.710
Storage values require caution.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:52.431
Data in localStorage or sessionStorage can be modified by the user or

00:00:52.431 --> 00:00:54.792
by scripts running in the page.

00:00:54.792 --> 00:00:59.120
Cookies can be changed unless they are protected and verified appropriately.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.236
Database records may have originated from older versions of the application, partner feeds,

00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:05.809
imports, or compromised accounts.

00:01:05.809 --> 00:01:08.170
Validation should focus on trust boundaries,

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:12.498
not only on whether a value came from a public form.

00:01:12.498 --> 00:01:17.220
A practical first step is to map where data enters the application

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.187
and where it goes next.

00:01:19.187 --> 00:01:23.909
For each input source, identify who controls it, how it is parsed,

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:27.844
what type and shape are expected, what business rules apply,

00:01:27.844 --> 00:01:29.811
and which code consumes it.

00:01:29.811 --> 00:01:34.533
That map helps developers choose the right validation point instead of scattering

00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:36.500
checks randomly across the codebase.
